WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:03.470
In the movie "Interstellar,"

00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:06.200
a landing craft explores
a planet near a black hole

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:09.693
that has a mass 100 million
times that of the Sun.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:13.140
The crew spend just a few
minutes on the planet,

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:14.890
but when they return to the main ship,

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:16.800
23 years have elapsed.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:17.980
I've waited years.

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:21.273
It's 23 years, four months, eight days.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:24.650
The idea of time dilation

00:00:24.650 --> 00:00:27.700
by going into deeper gravitational fields

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:29.933
is actually pretty accurate.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:33.970
First theorized by Einstein

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:38.373
in his theory of relativity,
it's known as time dilation.

00:00:39.918 --> 00:00:41.250
The very bizarre thing

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:44.660
is that when you're in a place
where gravity is stronger,

00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:46.313
time moves more slowly.

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:49.520
But that's not all.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:51.320
Another way time dilation occurs

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.773
is through your relative
movement through space.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:59.630
If you have two observers that have clocks

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:01.720
that tick at exactly the same rate,

00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:03.290
and then one of those observers

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:05.790
moves away at a very high speed,

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:08.250
the moving observer's clock
will tick more slowly.

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:11.530
Their time will literally pass slower.

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:13.250
And a lot of people are confused by this

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:14.700
'cause they think it might
be a mechanical thing.

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.670
It's the clock. But no, it
is in fact, literally time.

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:19.450
It doesn't matter what the clock is.

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:21.450
It can be a clock. It
could be your heartbeat.

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:22.403
It's time.

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:27.830
This is even true for
someone who is walking

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:30.490
compared with someone sitting at a table.

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:33.253
Time moves slower for the
person who is walking.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:37.400
Similarly, for
gravitational time dilation,

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:39.270
someone who is at sea level

00:01:39.270 --> 00:01:41.180
compared with someone on a mountain

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:44.590
will also experience
time at a slower rate.

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:46.450
That's because gravity is stronger

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:48.983
when you're closer to the
Earth's center of mass.

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:51.780
But in these examples,

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:54.580
the amount of time
dilation is so minuscule

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:56.223
that it's totally unnoticeable.

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:00.750
Apart from a few examples,

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:03.860
humans don't deal with fast
enough speeds or enough gravity

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:06.460
to experience a significant
amount of time dilation.

00:02:07.910 --> 00:02:09.650
But that may not matter.

00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:12.280
Because atomic clocks are
reaching a sensitivity

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:15.623
that can measure even the
tiniest amount of time dilation.

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:20.650
I think that's the place where relativity

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:22.820
will start to become far more important.

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:25.340
It's not because we can go so fast.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:26.940
I mean, I wish we could go fast,

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:28.480
but I think the real place

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:30.230
will be because we can measure things

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:32.571
with an incredible precision.

00:02:32.571 --> 00:02:33.463
That's amazing.

00:02:34.900 --> 00:02:36.350
That level of precision

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:37.720
will enable us to use time

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:40.380
in ways we never thought possible.

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:42.650
Like monitoring tectonic plates,

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:45.160
large movements deep
below Earth's surface,

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:46.363
and climate change.

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:49.750
Atomic clocks could even
lead to new discoveries

00:02:49.750 --> 00:02:51.923
regarding the nature of time itself.

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:55.350
I think that's one of the
really exciting things

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:59.480
about this, is that we're
really exploring the unknown

00:03:00.830 --> 00:03:04.290
or able to sense effects

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:07.200
that no one has been able to see before

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:09.093
using the precision of the clocks.

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:19.490
In 2010, two ultra-precise,

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:22.590
synchronized atomic clocks
were put next to each other.

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:26.430
These clocks were so
precise that they would lose

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:31.430
about one second over the
history of the entire Earth.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:33.290
Then one of them was raised

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:35.143
by just 33 centimeters.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:37.620
The result?

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:40.483
The clock that was higher
up began to tick faster.

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:43.840
But nothing was wrong with the clocks.

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:47.100
It was actually time that
sped up for the higher clock,

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:48.730
relative to the lower clock

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:50.740
due to the tiniest decrease in gravity

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:52.273
as the clock moved higher up.

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:57.880
Ultra-precise clocks measured
the effects of relativity

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:02.240
side by side, being changed by that much.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:04.873
Absolutely amazing, fantastic experiment.

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:08.590
Atomic clocks were used in the '70s

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:10.210
for that very purpose,

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:13.113
testing relativity and other
scientific experiments.

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:18.820
They flew some atomic clocks on airplanes

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:20.300
around the world,

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:24.900
compared the time before
and after doing that.

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:27.520
What they found confirmed
their predictions:

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:29.763
Time slowed down for the moving clocks.

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:33.720
The technology ended up
being integral to devices

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:36.923
that we depend on every day, GPS.

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:39.970
Global navigation satellite systems

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:42.770
depend on atomic clocks to
help determine where you are.

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:45.870
The time it takes for a signal

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:48.220
traveling at the speed of
light to reach your phone

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:50.400
will give you the distance.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:51.850
Using four or more of them,

00:04:51.850 --> 00:04:53.653
you can triangulate your position.

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:56.530
I really think of the atomic clocks

00:04:56.530 --> 00:05:00.520
as constantly working in the
background, you don't see it,

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:03.040
but atomic clocks are really important

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:04.590
for having the whole thing run.

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:09.030
The invention of GPS
meant that time dilation

00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:11.210
suddenly went from being a cool theory

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:14.160
to something that needed to be
dealt with on a daily basis.

00:05:15.770 --> 00:05:17.320
Due to the speed at which the satellites

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:21.120
are orbiting the Earth,
14,000 kilometers an hour,

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:23.220
and the lower gravity in space,

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:26.070
they are actually
experiencing time differently

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:27.573
relative to us on Earth.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:30.220
If you add everything up,

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:33.700
the clocks change by
about 40 microseconds,

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:35.070
40 millionths of a second of a day,

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:36.580
which sounds like nothing.

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:39.710
But if you did not take that
relativity into account,

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:43.060
every day, your position on your receiver

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:44.890
would say that you were 6 miles off

00:05:44.890 --> 00:05:46.600
from where you actually are.

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:49.803
So it would very quickly
be completely useless.

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:56.150
These dramatic effects of relativity

00:05:56.150 --> 00:05:57.810
are central to the work done here

00:05:57.810 --> 00:06:00.160
at the National Institute
of Standards and Technology

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:02.113
or NIST in Boulder, Colorado.

00:06:04.850 --> 00:06:06.220
NIST is a leader in the development

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:08.163
of optical atomic clocks.

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:12.010
These clocks use lasers to control atoms

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:13.703
and measure their oscillations.

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:18.010
Atomic clocks are currently

00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:19.590
the most stable and accurate way

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:21.510
of measuring time intervals.

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:26.510
And like any other clock, at
the heart of an atomic clock

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:30.520
is a very stable oscillator.

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:33.710
So you think about a grandfather clock.

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:37.640
It has a pendulum that serves
as the reference oscillator.

00:06:37.640 --> 00:06:39.900
It oscillates at one cycle per second.

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:41.630
An atomic clock is very similar.

00:06:41.630 --> 00:06:44.870
So at the heart of an atomic
clock is an oscillator,

00:06:44.870 --> 00:06:46.770
which is the atom itself.

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:49.710
Instead of having a
bunch of gears and levers

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:52.803
and springs and things,
it's a series of lasers.

00:06:54.950 --> 00:06:56.710
Atoms trapped in an electric field

00:06:56.710 --> 00:06:58.560
receive a series of pulses,

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:02.630
which aid in controlling and
measuring their oscillations.

00:07:02.630 --> 00:07:06.140
Instead of one tick per
second, these clocks oscillate

00:07:06.140 --> 00:07:09.240
or tick a million
billion times per second,

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:11.193
allowing for much more precision.

00:07:12.078 --> 00:07:15.290
So it's a really small inaccuracy.

00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:18.110
It's hard for anyone to conceptualize

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:20.950
that level of performance.

00:07:20.950 --> 00:07:23.210
These clocks are so reliable

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:26.090
that it would take more than
double the age of the universe,

00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:29.883
so around 30 billion years,
to drift by even a second.

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:33.170
That precision means relativity

00:07:33.170 --> 00:07:36.003
can also be measured
with incredible accuracy.

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:41.260
So if you move a clock up
by 1 centimeter of height,

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:46.260
it will tick faster by this
one part in 10 to the 18.

00:07:47.110 --> 00:07:51.253
And now we can detect that kind of change.

00:07:59.350 --> 00:08:00.780
Although this kind of precision

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:02.250
could be problematic

00:08:02.250 --> 00:08:04.600
since atomic clocks keep the official time

00:08:04.600 --> 00:08:06.803
that synchronize devices
all over the globe.

00:08:07.810 --> 00:08:09.410
That seems like a huge problem,

00:08:09.410 --> 00:08:11.730
I mean how do you decide
where to place the clocks

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:13.880
if the tiniest change in gravity

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:15.763
causes time to slow down or speed up?

00:08:16.630 --> 00:08:18.260
That is a good point

00:08:18.260 --> 00:08:22.020
and it's a problem that
we're sort of anticipating.

00:08:22.020 --> 00:08:25.540
Now the clocks are getting so good

00:08:25.540 --> 00:08:30.400
that we don't know their
gravitational potential well enough

00:08:30.400 --> 00:08:34.440
to define time globally
at that same level.

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:37.870
It does seem like a simpler environment

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:39.360
somewhere out in space,

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:43.213
farther away from these local
gravitational variations.

00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:48.200
The problem of determining
standard time aside,

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:50.670
this 1-centimeter level precision

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:52.340
has the potential to revolutionize

00:08:52.340 --> 00:08:54.240
the way things are measured,

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:57.490
using clocks to calculate
height and gravity

00:08:57.490 --> 00:08:59.340
based on the amount of time dilation.

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:03.010
This is particularly useful in geodesy,

00:09:03.010 --> 00:09:06.340
the study of the Earth's
shape, orientation in space

00:09:06.340 --> 00:09:07.543
and its gravity field.

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:13.400
We're studying the motion
of the crust of the Earth,

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:18.150
the plate tectonics, the tides,
the polar motion of Earth.

00:09:18.150 --> 00:09:20.340
There are many, many effects that observe

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:21.890
and that can be explained.

00:09:21.890 --> 00:09:24.520
For instance, in Sweden and Canada,

00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:27.660
it's rising on the order of
1 to 2 centimeters per year.

00:09:27.660 --> 00:09:29.090
That's quite a lot.

00:09:29.090 --> 00:09:32.170
And on another part of our planet,

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:34.320
there's actually a downward land movement.

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:37.710
So somewhere it's going up
and somewhere it's going down.

00:09:37.710 --> 00:09:40.620
And such effects, of
course, change everything

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:44.500
like the global sea level, earthquakes.

00:09:44.500 --> 00:09:48.003
They changed even the rotation
of the Earth and much more.

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:55.540
Understanding these movements

00:09:55.540 --> 00:09:58.000
can help aid in
understanding climate change,

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:01.010
natural hazards and
construction of major structures

00:10:01.010 --> 00:10:02.303
like bridges and roads.

00:10:04.010 --> 00:10:06.690
If atomic clocks were used in this field,

00:10:06.690 --> 00:10:09.200
they could in theory give
you height measurements

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:12.150
that are sensitive to just 1 centimeter.

00:10:12.150 --> 00:10:14.913
A level of accuracy GPS is not capable of.

00:10:16.090 --> 00:10:19.390
And because current methods
utilize GPS and a gravimeter

00:10:19.390 --> 00:10:21.880
for determining gravity at a single point,

00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:23.910
it doesn't always give you a clear picture

00:10:23.910 --> 00:10:24.913
of what's going on.

00:10:27.090 --> 00:10:30.184
Well, I'm from Germany, so
let's give you an example.

00:10:30.184 --> 00:10:32.560
In Germany, we're proud to
have our highest mountain.

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:36.163
It's called Zugspitze. It's
almost 3,000 meters high.

00:10:37.330 --> 00:10:40.780
But also this mountain
has permafrost underneath,

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:42.400
which is now melting.

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:46.610
And that actually gives rise
to a shrinking of the mountain.

00:10:46.610 --> 00:10:49.800
So the top of the mountain
is probably decreasing

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:52.503
by also 1 or 2 centimeters per year.

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:56.300
But we have not observed
or measured it yet

00:10:57.793 --> 00:11:00.483
because at that place if
you would put a gravimeter,

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:03.380
you would not see the signal

00:11:03.380 --> 00:11:06.380
because on a mountain you
have varying snow cover

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:08.350
from summer and winter.

00:11:08.350 --> 00:11:12.850
The snow is changing, the
groundwater level is changing,

00:11:12.850 --> 00:11:15.560
and all these local changes and mass

00:11:15.560 --> 00:11:18.230
are disguising the actual effect.

00:11:18.230 --> 00:11:20.340
So if you would combine
now your gravimeter

00:11:20.340 --> 00:11:23.090
with the clock on top of such a mountain,

00:11:23.090 --> 00:11:25.020
then you really could resolve what

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:28.800
the melting of the permafrost
is doing to the Alps

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:31.110
because the clock would not be sensitive

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:34.313
to a very local mass
change like snow cover.

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:38.680
But it would really
measure an averaged mass

00:11:38.680 --> 00:11:40.730
and gravitational potential

00:11:40.730 --> 00:11:43.530
and really show you the
physical height of the mountain.

00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:47.260
That's why geodesy missions
using atomic clocks

00:11:47.260 --> 00:11:49.793
are expected to take place
in the next few years.

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:52.920
So there's actually a mission in Germany,

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:55.070
which geodesists want to make,

00:11:55.070 --> 00:11:56.800
for the first time, a clock measurement.

00:11:56.800 --> 00:11:58.760
They want to bring a clock to Heligoland -

00:11:58.760 --> 00:12:02.380
this is the offshore island
in the Northern Sea -

00:12:03.740 --> 00:12:05.220
to compare and get the data

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:08.420
because at the moment this
island could not be leveled.

00:12:08.420 --> 00:12:10.490
It's too far out in the ocean

00:12:10.490 --> 00:12:12.550
and satellites would not resolve it.

00:12:12.550 --> 00:12:14.000
It's too small.

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:16.780
And that's why there's
right now a geodetic mission

00:12:16.780 --> 00:12:20.460
to bring a portable atomic
clock to this island

00:12:20.460 --> 00:12:23.503
and compare it to a continental clock.

00:12:25.610 --> 00:12:28.200
Heligoland's location and size

00:12:28.200 --> 00:12:30.550
make getting precise
height readouts difficult.

00:12:31.450 --> 00:12:35.430
Using GPS with gravimeter data
requires multiple readouts

00:12:35.430 --> 00:12:37.230
every 2 kilometers,

00:12:37.230 --> 00:12:39.760
so offshore areas or areas near a shore

00:12:39.760 --> 00:12:42.550
technically require you to
place a gravimeter in the ocean,

00:12:42.550 --> 00:12:43.993
which isn't possible.

00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:46.340
That's why the island's elevation

00:12:46.340 --> 00:12:48.773
hasn't been accurately
measured to this day.

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:53.820
Clocks can bridge the gaps to islands,

00:12:53.820 --> 00:12:56.040
deserts or mountainous areas,

00:12:56.040 --> 00:12:59.540
which are hard to access with
classical leveling methods.

00:12:59.540 --> 00:13:02.420
In turn, they can also act
as height reference points

00:13:02.420 --> 00:13:05.603
for understanding ocean
dynamics and global sea levels.

00:13:07.090 --> 00:13:09.400
But it's not all about geodesy.

00:13:09.400 --> 00:13:11.920
We may even learn things
about gravitational effects

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:13.843
that we don't entirely understand.

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:18.490
It's hard to predict what the
measurements will tell you,

00:13:18.490 --> 00:13:22.100
but certainly, we're up to many surprises.

00:13:22.100 --> 00:13:25.190
And this is actually the
boundary that we physicists

00:13:25.190 --> 00:13:26.330
are trying to push now,

00:13:26.330 --> 00:13:30.770
to measure more and more
precise time, frequency,

00:13:30.770 --> 00:13:32.300
space-time,

00:13:32.300 --> 00:13:35.230
as we know it's connected
in general relativity.

00:13:35.230 --> 00:13:37.010
Atomic clocks could be the key

00:13:37.010 --> 00:13:40.120
to answering some of the biggest
mysteries in physics today

00:13:40.120 --> 00:13:43.373
that shape the universe as
we know it, dark matter.

00:13:44.740 --> 00:13:46.820
We know that there's dark matter

00:13:46.820 --> 00:13:49.820
that's making up most of
the matter in the universe,

00:13:49.820 --> 00:13:53.520
and there are also models of dark matter

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:55.240
that can be searched for

00:13:55.240 --> 00:13:58.020
and constrained using atomic clocks.

00:13:58.020 --> 00:14:00.483
So there are a lot of
scientific applications.

00:14:01.950 --> 00:14:04.450
As clocks get more precise, we could find

00:14:04.450 --> 00:14:07.942
that constants used in everyday
physics equations are wrong.

00:14:07.942 --> 00:14:09.722
And that could completely change

00:14:09.722 --> 00:14:11.580
how we understand the universe.

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:13.190
Atomic clocks that we're building

00:14:13.190 --> 00:14:15.500
are the most sensitive way

00:14:15.500 --> 00:14:17.950
to look for this present-day variation

00:14:17.950 --> 00:14:20.390
in the fundamental constants.

00:14:20.390 --> 00:14:25.060
It really has the potential
to revolutionize physics.

00:14:25.060 --> 00:14:29.680
So do you think atomic clocks could lead

00:14:29.680 --> 00:14:32.193
to an Einstein-esque discovery about time?

00:14:33.290 --> 00:14:36.020
I think it's certainly possible.

00:14:36.020 --> 00:14:40.985
If we did detect that there was a drift

00:14:40.985 --> 00:14:44.070
in the fundamental constants over time,

00:14:44.070 --> 00:14:46.000
that would be a revolution

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:50.070
similar to the discovery of relativity

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:53.183
or the discovery of quantum mechanics.

00:14:55.450 --> 00:14:57.890
Basically, we're doing a lot of tests

00:14:57.890 --> 00:15:00.710
on the predictions of Albert Einstein

00:15:00.710 --> 00:15:02.330
because can we really assume

00:15:02.330 --> 00:15:05.610
that general relativity is the sole theory

00:15:05.610 --> 00:15:09.460
explaining everything or
is there maybe a deviation

00:15:09.460 --> 00:15:12.273
from the predictions that
general relativity makes?

00:15:17.520 --> 00:15:18.830
The fact is, I don't think

00:15:18.830 --> 00:15:21.720
we do understand time very well.

00:15:21.720 --> 00:15:24.120
It's always possible
that there is going to be

00:15:24.120 --> 00:15:26.570
some bright young person out there

00:15:26.570 --> 00:15:31.570
who's really terribly disturbed
by the conundrum of time

00:15:31.880 --> 00:15:32.713
and thinking it over

00:15:32.713 --> 00:15:34.320
and might come up with
something brilliant.

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:37.550
But it's going to require a
different way of thinking.

00:15:37.550 --> 00:15:40.160
It's going to require
that flash a-ha moment

00:15:40.160 --> 00:15:43.973
that happens once per century
or even once per millennia.

